    {% if can_manage %}
    <div class="card" style="border-color:{{ site.smtpHost ? 'var(--border)' : 'rgba(245,158,11,.45)' }}">
      <details {{ site.smtpHost ? '' : 'open' }}>
        <summary style="font-weight:600;font-size:1.05rem">
          Email (SMTP)
          {% if site.smtpHost %}
            <span class="mut" style="font-weight:400">— {{ site.smtpHost }}</span>
          {% else %}
            <span class="badge warn" style="font-weight:400">not configured</span>
          {% endif %}
        </summary>
        <p class="mut" style="font-size:.86rem;margin:.6rem 0 .8rem;line-height:1.6">
          A published site is static, so it cannot send anything itself. A form on it posts here and this server relays the message through
          <strong>your own mail account</strong> — so notifications leave your domain, keeping your SPF and DKIM alignment, and no lead
          ever rides on someone else's sending reputation. <strong>Until this is filled in, forms still collect and store every submission,
          but nobody is notified.</strong>
        </p>
        <form method="post" action="/sites/{{ site.handle }}/settings/action" class="stack">
          <input type="hidden" name="_csrf" value="{{ csrf }}"><input type="hidden" name="tab" value="{{ tab }}">
          <div class="row">
            <div>
              <label>Host</label>
              <input type="text" name="smtp_host" value="{{ site.smtpHost }}" placeholder="smtp.example.com" autocomplete="off">
            </div>
            <div class="tight">
              <label>Encryption</label>
              <select name="smtp_encryption">
                <option value="starttls" {{ site.smtpEncryption == 'starttls' ? 'selected' }}>STARTTLS (587)</option>
                <option value="tls" {{ site.smtpEncryption == 'tls' ? 'selected' }}>TLS (465)</option>
                <option value="none" {{ site.smtpEncryption == 'none' ? 'selected' }}>None (25)</option>
              </select>
            </div>
            <div class="tight">
              <label>Port <span class="mut">(0 = default)</span></label>
              <input type="number" name="smtp_port" value="{{ site.smtpPort }}" min="0" max="65535" size="5">
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="row">
            <div>
              <label>Username</label>
              <input type="text" name="smtp_username" value="{{ site.smtpUsername }}" autocomplete="off">
            </div>
            <div>
              <label>Password</label>
              <input type="password" name="smtp_password" autocomplete="new-password"
                     placeholder="{{ site.smtpPasswordEnc ? '••••••••  (saved — leave blank to reuse)' : 'mailbox or app password' }}">
            </div>
          </div>
          {% if site.smtpPasswordEnc %}<label class="mut" style="font-size:.85rem"><input type="checkbox" name="smtp_password_clear" value="1"> remove the saved password</label>{% endif %}
          <div class="row">
            <div>
              <label>Send from</label>
              <input type="email" name="smtp_from_email" value="{{ site.smtpFromEmail }}" placeholder="website@example.com" autocomplete="off">
            </div>
            <div>
              <label>Sender name</label>
              <input type="text" name="smtp_from_name" value="{{ site.smtpFromName }}" placeholder="{{ site.name }}" autocomplete="off">
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="mut" style="font-size:.82rem;margin:.2rem 0 0;line-height:1.55">
            <strong>“Send from” must be an address this account is allowed to send as</strong> — usually the mailbox itself. It is never the
            visitor's address: providers reject, or silently spam-file, a sender they did not authenticate. The person who filled the form in
            goes into <span class="mono">Reply-To</span>, so hitting reply still answers them.
          </p>
          <label style="font-size:.9rem;margin-top:.6rem"><input type="checkbox" name="smtp_verify_cert" value="1" {{ site.smtpVerifyCert ? 'checked' }}> Verify the server's TLS certificate</label>
          <p class="mut" style="font-size:.82rem;margin:.2rem 0 0 1.5rem;line-height:1.55">
            Leave this on where you can — your mailbox password travels over this connection. Untick it only for a mail server whose
            certificate is self-signed or issued for its own hostname, which is common on shared hosting. The connection stays encrypted
            either way; what you give up is proof of who is on the other end.
          </p>
          <div class="row" style="margin-top:.8rem;align-items:flex-end">
            <div>
              <label>Send a test to</label>
              <input type="email" name="smtp_test_to" placeholder="{{ user.email }}" autocomplete="off">
            </div>
            <button type="submit" name="action" value="smtp-test" class="btn-secondary" data-busy="Sending…">Save &amp; send test email</button>
            <button type="submit" name="action" value="smtp-save" data-busy="Saving…">Save only</button>
          </div>
        </form>
        <p class="mut" style="font-size:.82rem;margin:.8rem 0 0;line-height:1.6">
          <strong>Microsoft 365 and Google Workspace both need more than the normal account password.</strong> Google requires an
          <em>app password</em>, which needs 2-step verification switched on first. Microsoft disables SMTP AUTH by default on new
          tenants and it has to be enabled per mailbox by an administrator. If the test comes back “the server rejected the username
          or password”, that is almost always what it is — not a typo.
        </p>
        <p class="mut" style="font-size:.82rem;margin:.6rem 0 0;line-height:1.6">
          The test sends a <em>real</em> message rather than only logging in, because a server can accept the credentials and still refuse
          to deliver as that sender. If it arrives, form notifications will arrive.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
